content=intro('Personal study tool / Web application','SAT Vocabulary Mastery','I wanted vocabulary practice to connect testing, my own memory notes, and the next review. I built an app that brings those steps into one study loop.','Actively used prototype','React · TypeScript · Firestore')
content+=section('01 / The problem','A definition alone was not the feedback I wanted','<p>Vocabulary study can become a cycle of rereading lists and recognizing familiar words without reliably recalling their meaning. Personal memory notes are less useful when they sit somewhere separate from the quiz.</p><p>I wanted the moment after answering a question to reconnect the word with my saved association, then set up the next review. That became the starting point for Vocabulary Mastery.</p>')
content+=section('02 / What I built','Bring the memory note into the quiz',flow(['Choose a word and answer a question','Review the answer and saved mnemonic','Update the word’s review interval','Return when another review is due']))
content+='<div class="app-notes">'+card('My notes at the point of review','After either a correct or incorrect answer, the review drawer brings the saved Memory Lab note alongside contextual reference material.')+card('A queue instead of an equal repeat','An SM-2-based scheduler updates word progress and the next review date, helping organize what to revisit.')+card('One place to build associations','Memory Lab keeps personal mnemonic notes connected to the word and makes them available during later practice.')+'</div>'
content+=section('03 / Why it helps my workflow','Less moving between lists, notes, and quizzes','<p>The vocabulary library, practice screen, memory notes, and review schedule share the same word state. I can work on an association and see it again when testing that word, rather than searching a separate notebook.</p><p>The app also groups words by mastery tier and lists items due for review. These are tools for organizing study; the project does not establish a measured improvement in SAT scores or long-term retention.</p>')
content+=section('04 / A change driven by the problem','Show my memory trick after every answer','<p>Originally, mnemonic notes were visible only in Memory Lab. The quiz showed correctness and dictionary information, leaving the personal association outside the feedback loop.</p><p>I connected the saved notes to the post-answer drawer for both correct and incorrect responses. For a word without a saved note, a link leads back to Memory Lab. I checked the behavior across words with and without notes.</p>')
content+=section('05 / Engineering challenge','Prevent cloud updates from writing themselves back','<p>A local progress change could trigger a cloud write, whose snapshot listener updated local state and triggered another write. I added guards that compare the serialized payload with the last saved or received state, alongside a 1.5-second debounce.</p><p>This reduces redundant writes during study. Local storage provides a fallback when cloud access is unavailable; loading the entire app offline remains a separate planned feature.</p>')
content+=role('I designed the practice and Memory Lab workflow, implemented the SM-2-based scheduling logic and review drawer, worked on cloud synchronization and responsive layouts, and curated the vocabulary dataset described in the brief.','AI assistance supported initial scaffolding and optional seed mnemonic ideas. I reviewed and revised the application logic, error states, and layouts.')
content+=section('Current scope','A working study tool with room to improve','<div class="app-notes two">'+card('Implemented','Vocabulary search, quizzes, personal mnemonic notes, review scheduling, mastery views, browser pronunciation, and local/cloud progress storage.')+card('Next steps','Custom decks, passage-based practice, and fuller offline support. No runtime screenshots were included with this brief, so no app screen is simulated here.')+'</div><p class="app-attribution">Scheduling is based on the SuperMemo SM-2 concept by Piotr Wozniak. The source brief identifies Cambridge Dictionary as the contextual reference source.</p><p class="app-links"><a href="https://ais-pre-6xntpdpwybnlej5p2siucy-929692918415.asia-east1.run.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open Vocabulary Mastery ↗</a></p>')
(out/'software-vocabulary.html').write_text(page('SAT Vocabulary Mastery','A personal vocabulary study app connecting quizzes, mnemonic notes, and spaced review.',content),encoding='utf-8')
